WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.723
Strata started in June 2014.

2
00:00:04.103 --> 00:00:06.329
Today it holds almost nine hundred

3
00:00:06.329 --> 00:00:08.683
thousand lines of code, in more than

4
00:00:08.683 --> 00:00:11.036
four thousand eight hundred commits.

5
00:00:11.416 --> 00:00:14.195
It is OpenGamma's open source library

6
00:00:14.195 --> 00:00:17.560
for pricing trades and measuring market risk.

7
00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:19.849
Over the next few minutes, we will

8
00:00:19.849 --> 00:00:21.759
walk through how it fits together.

9
00:00:23.039 --> 00:00:25.364
First, what does Strata actually do?

10
00:00:25.744 --> 00:00:28.048
You give it trades and market data.

11
00:00:28.428 --> 00:00:31.587
It gives you numbers like present value,

12
00:00:31.587 --> 00:00:33.975
PV01, and curve sensitivities.

13
00:00:34.355 --> 00:00:37.439
Applications embed it as a Java library,

14
00:00:37.439 --> 00:00:39.997
and it is the core of OpenGamma's

15
00:00:39.997 --> 00:00:41.501
own SaaS analytics.

16
00:00:41.881 --> 00:00:44.841
The API is kept backwards compatible, so

17
00:00:44.841 --> 00:00:47.151
those users can upgrade safely.

18
00:00:48.431 --> 00:00:50.934
The code is one Maven build, with

19
00:00:50.934 --> 00:00:53.879
eleven library modules and one examples

20
00:00:53.879 --> 00:00:54.468
module.

21
00:00:54.848 --> 00:00:56.875
You can think of them as layers.

22
00:00:57.255 --> 00:00:59.914
At the bottom, collect and basics hold

23
00:00:59.914 --> 00:01:02.232
utilities, currencies, dates, and

24
00:01:02.232 --> 00:01:03.527
holiday calendars.

25
00:01:03.907 --> 00:01:06.356
In the middle sit the domain models:

26
00:01:06.356 --> 00:01:09.069
market data, products, math, and curves.

27
00:01:09.449 --> 00:01:12.273
On top, pricer values products, calc

28
00:01:12.273 --> 00:01:15.785
runs calculations, and measure joins the two.

29
00:01:17.065 --> 00:01:18.836
Next, let's price something.

30
00:01:19.216 --> 00:01:22.085
The FX pricing example does the whole

31
00:01:22.085 --> 00:01:24.123
job in about thirty lines.

32
00:01:24.503 --> 00:01:27.623
It builds four FX trades and asks

33
00:01:27.623 --> 00:01:31.293
for three columns: present value, PV01,

34
00:01:31.293 --> 00:01:33.036
and bucketed PV01.

35
00:01:33.416 --> 00:01:36.069
A calculation runner takes the rules,

36
00:01:36.069 --> 00:01:38.443
trades, columns, market data, and

37
00:01:38.443 --> 00:01:39.560
reference data.

38
00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:42.219
Out comes a grid of results, with

39
00:01:42.219 --> 00:01:44.633
one cell for each trade and column.

40
00:01:45.913 --> 00:01:48.563
Inside the runner, the calc module plans

41
00:01:48.563 --> 00:01:50.244
the work before doing it.

42
00:01:50.624 --> 00:01:52.890
Each trade and column becomes a

43
00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:54.165
calculation task.

44
00:01:54.545 --> 00:01:57.511
Every task declares the market data it needs.

45
00:01:57.891 --> 00:02:00.732
A market data factory builds that data

46
00:02:00.732 --> 00:02:03.864
up front, including any calibrated curves.

47
00:02:04.244 --> 00:02:06.633
Then the tasks run on a thread pool.

48
00:02:07.013 --> 00:02:09.723
Failures become Result objects, so one

49
00:02:09.723 --> 00:02:12.155
bad trade does not stop the batch.

50
00:02:13.435 --> 00:02:15.269
Now look at the trade side.

51
00:02:15.649 --> 00:02:18.403
The product module is the largest, with

52
00:02:18.403 --> 00:02:21.020
four hundred thirty five source files

53
00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:23.223
across every major asset class.

54
00:02:23.603 --> 00:02:26.124
Before pricing, a trade is resolved

55
00:02:26.124 --> 00:02:27.805
against reference data.

56
00:02:28.185 --> 00:02:31.148
Resolution turns conventions and holiday

57
00:02:31.148 --> 00:02:33.967
calendars into concrete payment dates.

58
00:02:34.347 --> 00:02:37.096
You can remember this as: resolve once,

59
00:02:37.096 --> 00:02:38.677
then price many times.

60
00:02:39.957 --> 00:02:42.859
The pricer module is where the maths lives.

61
00:02:43.239 --> 00:02:44.711
Pricers are layered.

62
00:02:45.091 --> 00:02:47.764
A trade pricer calls a product pricer,

63
00:02:47.764 --> 00:02:50.232
which calls leg and period pricers.

64
00:02:50.612 --> 00:02:53.091
At the bottom, every pricer reads rates

65
00:02:53.091 --> 00:02:54.516
from a rates provider.

66
00:02:54.896 --> 00:02:57.459
Sensitivities travel back up the same

67
00:02:57.459 --> 00:02:59.483
path, as point sensitivities.

68
00:03:00.763 --> 00:03:03.344
Curves usually do not arrive ready made.

69
00:03:03.724 --> 00:03:05.974
A curve group definition lists the

70
00:03:05.974 --> 00:03:08.417
market instruments behind each curve.

71
00:03:08.797 --> 00:03:11.005
In the euro example, that means

72
00:03:11.005 --> 00:03:13.766
overnight swaps, basis swaps, FRAs, and

73
00:03:13.766 --> 00:03:15.560
fixed for floating swaps.

74
00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:18.224
The rates curve calibrator solves for

75
00:03:18.224 --> 00:03:20.569
curves that reprice those instruments.

76
00:03:20.949 --> 00:03:23.434
Point sensitivities are then mapped onto

77
00:03:23.434 --> 00:03:24.768
the curve parameters.

78
00:03:25.148 --> 00:03:28.021
That is how Strata produces bucketed

79
00:03:28.021 --> 00:03:30.972
PV01, one number for each curve node.

80
00:03:32.252 --> 00:03:35.879
Much of Strata's vocabulary is data, not code.

81
00:03:36.259 --> 00:03:38.818
Day counts, indices, and holiday

82
00:03:38.818 --> 00:03:41.144
calendars are extended enums.

83
00:03:41.524 --> 00:03:44.204
Their values load from INI files on

84
00:03:44.204 --> 00:03:45.321
the classpath.

85
00:03:45.701 --> 00:03:48.729
Applications can add their own values in

86
00:03:48.729 --> 00:03:51.461
library or application configuration

87
00:03:51.461 --> 00:03:52.421
directories.

88
00:03:52.801 --> 00:03:55.208
The key point is that new conventions

89
00:03:55.208 --> 00:03:56.919
do not need a code change.

90
00:03:58.199 --> 00:04:00.828
Real systems also need data in and

91
00:04:00.828 --> 00:04:01.804
reports out.

92
00:04:02.184 --> 00:04:04.636
The loader module reads trades,

93
00:04:04.636 --> 00:04:07.011
positions, quotes, curves, and

94
00:04:07.011 --> 00:04:09.309
sensitivities from CSV files.

95
00:04:09.689 --> 00:04:13.231
It also parses FpML trade documents.

96
00:04:13.611 --> 00:04:16.061
At the other end, the report module

97
00:04:16.061 --> 00:04:18.715
turns results into trade and cash flow

98
00:04:18.715 --> 00:04:19.328
reports.

99
00:04:19.708 --> 00:04:22.918
Report layouts are INI templates, and

100
00:04:22.918 --> 00:04:26.044
output can be an ASCII table or CSV.

101
00:04:27.324 --> 00:04:29.820
This branch makes one large change.

102
00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:33.160
The collect and basics modules are now

103
00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:34.829
written in Scala 3.9.

104
00:04:35.209 --> 00:04:37.229
That is two hundred thirty three

105
00:04:37.229 --> 00:04:39.555
production files, converted in twenty

106
00:04:39.555 --> 00:04:40.351
six commits.

107
00:04:40.731 --> 00:04:43.055
The key point is that their Java

108
00:04:43.055 --> 00:04:44.464
API did not change.

109
00:04:44.844 --> 00:04:47.734
Java modules and outside users compile

110
00:04:47.734 --> 00:04:50.551
against the same classes, signatures,

111
00:04:50.551 --> 00:04:52.033
and static methods.

112
00:04:52.413 --> 00:04:55.130
Two small build steps restore details

113
00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:57.704
that Scala cannot express directly.

114
00:04:58.084 --> 00:05:00.217
The tests stayed in Java, so they

115
00:05:00.217 --> 00:05:02.287
check the Java view of the code.

116
00:05:03.567 --> 00:05:05.743
Here is the codebase by the numbers.

117
00:05:06.123 --> 00:05:08.832
Java is eighty three percent of all lines.

118
00:05:09.212 --> 00:05:11.647
Scala is now eight percent, all of

119
00:05:11.647 --> 00:05:14.012
it in the two foundation modules.

120
00:05:14.392 --> 00:05:16.435
Test code is about half the size

121
00:05:16.435 --> 00:05:17.549
of the main code.

122
00:05:17.929 --> 00:05:20.564
The pricer module is tested hardest,

123
00:05:20.564 --> 00:05:23.839
with almost as many test lines as code lines.

124
00:05:25.119 --> 00:05:26.996
The history has a clear shape.

125
00:05:27.376 --> 00:05:29.746
Most of the library was built in

126
00:05:29.746 --> 00:05:32.475
2015, with more than nineteen hundred

127
00:05:32.475 --> 00:05:33.840
commits that year.

128
00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:37.569
Version one shipped in July 2016.

129
00:05:37.949 --> 00:05:40.509
Since then the project has settled into

130
00:05:40.509 --> 00:05:42.685
a steady rhythm, with one hundred

131
00:05:42.685 --> 00:05:44.605
seventy nine releases so far.

132
00:05:44.985 --> 00:05:47.681
Recent change is concentrated in the

133
00:05:47.681 --> 00:05:49.721
measure and loader modules.

134
00:05:51.001 --> 00:05:53.227
If you want to contribute, start with

135
00:05:53.227 --> 00:05:54.223
the conventions.

136
00:05:54.603 --> 00:05:57.298
Types are immutable, and most of them

137
00:05:57.298 --> 00:05:59.851
are Joda-Beans with generated code.

138
00:06:00.231 --> 00:06:03.239
Never edit inside the generated blocks.

139
00:06:03.619 --> 00:06:06.067
Before a pull request, run the strict

140
00:06:06.067 --> 00:06:06.904
Maven build.

141
00:06:07.284 --> 00:06:09.930
It adds the enforcer and Joda-Beans

142
00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:13.385
validation on top of Checkstyle and the tests.

143
00:06:14.665 --> 00:06:15.711
To recap.

144
00:06:16.091 --> 00:06:18.895
Collect and basics are the foundation,

145
00:06:18.895 --> 00:06:21.915
now in Scala with an unchanged Java face.

146
00:06:22.295 --> 00:06:24.879
Products resolve, pricers value them,

147
00:06:24.879 --> 00:06:27.735
and the calc engine runs it all at scale.

148
00:06:28.115 --> 00:06:31.592
A good first step is the FX pricing example.

149
00:06:31.972 --> 00:06:34.145
Run it, then follow one trade through

150
00:06:34.145 --> 00:06:34.831
the layers.
